e function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(3,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186380851 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186380850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380846 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e22d5e18-f2d7-5ad6-beaf-4cf2d9f87028" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380847 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380847 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380847 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bb4515cd-9e47-5ff7-b9db-80d4caff3722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380848 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380848 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380848 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "9fd2179a-393c-5d9d-935b-1e1aabd85a88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380849 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380849 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380849 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "294b19d4-4da4-5a2d-964c-fb0115fffed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380850 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380850 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380850 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d683d66-22db-5f8a-9a0c-c12b9c67e331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380851 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380851 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380851 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fc2be26d-1186-5a98-9d33-d6449a993930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186382401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80536" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${∬}_{R}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-2,2]$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47830ab2-2bae-52c6-8c9c-df89a69a6369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382400 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/5c/5c5dbc673c2c6a9bd0a4f06223133e21e2dcbbdbaa2dfe2de2b29d8824dd821e.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+y}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$ Also, the integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statements II and III are true. In both cases, the rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186382401